.TH yamn 1 "21 Oct 2026" "version 0.2.7+gw1"

.SH NAME
yamn - An anonymous remailer application

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B [-m] [-s] [-l rem1,rem2,rem3,...] [-t user@host] [-s subject] [-c num] [recipient] filename
.PP
.B [-M] [-D] [-s]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Yamn (Yet another Mix network) is  an  anonymous  remailer.  Remailers  provide
protection against   traffic  analysis  and  allow  sending  mail  anonymously
or pseudonymously.

In the non-interactive mode, Yamn reads a message from  its  standard  input
or from a Maildir.  Destination address and input file can be specified in the
command line.  If no address is given in  the  command line,  the  input  file
is expected to contain a message complete with mail headers.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "-c, --copies=\fInum"
When operating in client mode, define how many copies of each message should be
sent (1 to 5).  Multiple copies share the same exit-remailer which retains a list of
processed Packet-IDs, ensuring that only a single copy is delivered.  This setting overrides the
config option
.B "stats.num_copies"
(default: 2).
.TP
.B "--config=\fIfilename"
Use this configuration file.  See
.B CONFIGURATION
for where yamn looks without this option.
.TP
.B "-d, --dummy"
Send a dummy message through a random chain.
.TP
.B "--debug"
Print the configuration in use, with all defaults filled in, and exit.
.TP
.B "--dir=\fIdirectory"
Base directory for yamn's files: the default paths of the
.B files
config options are relative to it, and
.I directory/yamn.yml
is one of the places the configuration is looked for.
.TP
.B "-D, --daemon"
Start a remailer in an endless loop of reading, processing and sending
messages.  This option only has meaning when used with the
.B "-M"
option.
.TP
.B "--keep-headers"
In client mode, send the message's headers as they are.  Without it, yamn
removes headers that could identify you before encrypting the message: anything
an exit wouldn't pass on anyway, plus Date and Message-ID, and the name in
From.  It reports what it removed.
.TP
.B "-l, --chain=\fIrem1,rem2,rem3,..."
Use the defined chain to route the message through the Yamn network.  Random
nodes can be selected with asterisks. E.g. --chain="*,*,*".
If multiple copies are specified, all copies must share a common exit remailer.
.TP
.B "-m, --mail"
Operate in client mode and inject a plain-text message into the Yamn pool
.TP
.B "-M, --remailer"
Operate in server mode.  Normally used in combination with 
.B "-D"
to continuously process, otherwise only a single iteration of read, process,
send  will be performed.
.TP
.B "--meminfo"
In server mode, log the process's memory use when it starts and with the
hourly statistics (needs loglevel info).
.TP
.B "--nodummy"
Don't send dummy messages.
.TP
.B "-R, --read-mail"
Read an incoming mail from STDIN and store it in the remailer's Maildir, as an
MTA would deliver it.  STDIN may also hold an mbox of several mails, such as
the output of
.BR --stdout ,
which is split into one Maildir message per mail.
.TP
.B "--refresh"
Download the public keyring and statistics now (from
.B urls.pubring
and
.BR urls.mlist2 ,
or from every pinger in
.BR urls.pingers )
and exit.
.TP
.B "-s, --subject=\fIsubject"
Specify a Subject header for the message.  If this isn't defined, the Subject
is expected to be defined within the message.
.TP
.B "-S, --send"
Flush the outbound pool.  Useful for client mode and remailer testing but
should not be used on an in-production remailer.
.TP
.B "--stdout"
Write the encrypted mails to STDOUT instead of storing them in the Pool, so
they can be sent some other way.  The output is an mbox: one mail per copy and
chunk, each addressed to its first remailer.  No dummy message is sent.  A
remailer can read this output with
.BR -R .
.TP
.B "--import"
Read encrypted mails from STDIN, as written by
.BR --stdout ,
and store them in the Pool, to be sent with
.BR -S .
STDIN may hold an mbox or a single mail.  Each mail must carry a valid yamn
packet; others are reported and skipped.  This allows encrypting a message on
one machine and sending it from another, which never sees the plaintext:
.B "yamn -m --stdout < msg > out.mbox"
on the first,
.B "yamn --import -S < out.mbox"
on the second.
.TP
.B "-t, --to=\fIuser@host"
Specify a recipient for the message.  If this option isn't defined, the recipient
is expected to be included in the message itself.
.TP
.B "-V, --version"
Print the version and exit.  Versions of this fork are the upstream
(crooks/yamn) release they are based on plus
.RI +gw N ,
e.g. 0.2.7+gw1.  Encrypted mails carry only the upstream version, so
they don't tell which fork sent them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In client mode
.RB ( -m ),
yamn exits with 0 when the whole message was pooled (or written with
.BR --stdout ),
and with 1 when it was refused: for example an unknown remailer or a chain
that can't be built, no To, Cc or Bcc address, or more than two arguments.
Every chunk and copy is encrypted before the first one is pooled, so after
an error nothing of the message is sent.  A dummy message that can't be
built only gives a warning.
.SH CONFIGURATION
yamn reads a YAML configuration file.  It uses the first one it finds of:
.IP 1. 4
the file given with
.BR --config ;
.IP 2. 4
the file named by the
.B YAMNCFG
environment variable;
.IP 3. 4
.I yamn.yml
in the
.B --dir
directory, if
.B --dir
is given;
.IP 4. 4
.IR ~/yamn.yml ;
.IP 5. 4
.IR /etc/yamn.yml .
.PP
A
.I yamn.yml
in the current directory is not read automatically (it could be planted by
someone else); use
.B "--config ./yamn.yml"
for that.  Without any config file, yamn uses its built-in defaults.
.PP
A config file only needs the options that differ from the defaults.  Options
are grouped in sections, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
remailer:
    name: myremailer
    address: myremailer@example.org
    exit: true
mail:
    smtp_relay: localhost
    mx_relay: false
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every option, with its default and a short description, is listed in
.IR examples/yamn.yml.defaults ;
.I examples/yamn.yml.client
and
.I examples/yamn.yml.remailer
are minimal starting points.
.B "yamn --debug"
prints the configuration in effect.
.SS Several pingers
By default the keyring and statistics come from a single pinger
.RB ( urls.pubring
and
.BR urls.mlist2 ).
That pinger decides which key each remailer has.  With
.BR urls.pingers ,
yamn downloads the files of several pingers, kept as
.I pubring.mix.<name>
and
.IR mlist2.txt.<name> ,
and merges them into the usual
.I pubring.mix
and
.IR mlist2.txt :
.IP \(bu 2
A key is only used if at least
.B urls.key_quorum
pingers (default 2) publish it for the same remailer.  So one wrong, stale or
compromised pinger can neither add a key nor replace one.  If fewer pingers
are working, all of them must agree.
.IP \(bu 2
A remailer name must belong to one address; if pingers disagree on that, the
address published by more pingers is used.
.IP \(bu 2
Statistics are the median of the pingers whose
.I mlist2.txt
is at most
.B stats.stale_hours
old, taking the higher latency and lower uptime when there are two middle
values.  The merged file carries the oldest Generated time used.
.PP
Disagreements between pingers are logged (loglevel info), problems with a
pinger as warnings.  A pinger that can't be reached keeps its last downloaded
files.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I ISSUES.md
in the source tree for known issues and their status.
